WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:03.493
[SWOOSH]

00:00:06.487 --> 00:00:08.990
[HEARTBEAT]

00:00:08.990 --> 00:00:13.200
NARRATOR: A strange invisible
assassin stalks its prey.

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:16.940
It attacks and hijacks the
cell forcing it to produce

00:00:16.940 --> 00:00:18.110
hundreds of new raiders.

00:00:20.540 --> 00:00:24.530
This is what happens when
the microcosmos turns on us.

00:00:24.530 --> 00:00:28.160
In 2020, we experienced
this invasion of the body

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:29.960
snatchers first hand.

00:00:31.290 --> 00:00:36.660
A tiny virus caused a
global pandemic, COVID-19.

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:38.550
HAKEEM OLUSEYI: This pandemic
makes me see the world

00:00:38.550 --> 00:00:40.680
completely differently
because there

00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:44.250
was an invisible universe, one
that covers me and is within me

00:00:44.250 --> 00:00:46.980
that I pretty much
ignored for the most part.

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:50.880
NARRATOR: Maybe we ignore this
other universe because it's

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:52.250
invisible to the naked eye.

00:00:53.686 --> 00:00:56.470
PHIL PLAIT: We live in a
largely invisible universe.

00:00:56.470 --> 00:00:58.810
We can't see everything
that's going on.

00:00:58.810 --> 00:01:01.280
Some of the biggest stuff
we can't see very well.

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:03.070
And in fact, a lot of
the littlest stuff,

00:01:03.070 --> 00:01:04.569
we can't either.

00:01:04.569 --> 00:01:07.120
Just because something
is too small to see

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:08.780
doesn't mean it's not important.

00:01:08.780 --> 00:01:11.350
In fact, very, very
tiny things have

00:01:11.350 --> 00:01:13.110
a huge impact on our lives.

00:01:13.110 --> 00:01:14.665
[SWOOSH]

00:01:14.665 --> 00:01:17.120
NARRATOR: Some of these
impacts are beneficial

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:20.270
but they're overshadowed by the
devastating effects of disease.

00:01:22.055 --> 00:01:23.720
LEWIS DARTNELL:
The longest running

00:01:23.720 --> 00:01:26.810
conflict that humanity
has engaged in

00:01:26.810 --> 00:01:29.210
is that against the microcosmos.

00:01:29.210 --> 00:01:33.950
Our long running battle
against bacteria and viruses,

00:01:33.950 --> 00:01:34.820
they're trying to kill us.

00:01:36.110 --> 00:01:38.870
NARRATOR: A century ago,
we faced the Spanish flu.

00:01:39.676 --> 00:01:42.290
CARLY HOWETT: This absolutely
annihilated Europe.

00:01:42.290 --> 00:01:44.960
At the time, it caused millions
upon millions of deaths.

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:47.360
So this is definitely
not the first pandemic,

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:49.760
it quite frankly, probably
isn't the last one.

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:52.460
 In a way, the
Coronavirus has forced

00:01:52.460 --> 00:01:54.500
us to come to a
greater understanding

00:01:54.500 --> 00:01:55.760
of the microscopic world.

00:01:57.690 --> 00:02:00.120
NARRATOR: The
microcosmos is complex.

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:04.550
While some parts kill
us, others keep us alive.

00:02:04.550 --> 00:02:07.650
CARLY HOWETT: The microcosmos
is important for us to survive,

00:02:07.650 --> 00:02:09.867
in order for us to
do all of the things

00:02:09.867 --> 00:02:11.100
that our bodies
does, in order for us

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:13.360
to live and grow and thrive.

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:16.020
 Our lives depend
on the microcosmos.

00:02:16.020 --> 00:02:19.890
The air we breathe and the
surface of the earth around us

00:02:19.890 --> 00:02:22.740
has been profoundly
affected by the microcosmos.

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:23.985
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:02:23.985 --> 00:02:26.640
NARRATOR: The invisible
microcosmos operates

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:28.830
in every part of the world.

00:02:28.830 --> 00:02:31.560
 This world that you
can't see is everywhere

00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:36.030
from geothermal hot springs,
to glaciers, to deep sea

00:02:36.030 --> 00:02:37.455
hydrothermal vent systems.

00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:42.030
NARRATOR: We have discovered
that every cubic foot

00:02:42.030 --> 00:02:44.130
of Earth's atmosphere
and its oceans

00:02:44.130 --> 00:02:45.600
contain millions of viruses.

00:02:47.170 --> 00:02:50.130
JANE RADEBAUGH: There are
more viruses here on Earth

00:02:50.130 --> 00:02:53.100
than there are stars
in the universe.

00:02:53.100 --> 00:02:53.910
That's incredible.

00:02:53.910 --> 00:02:55.230
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:02:55.230 --> 00:02:57.510
NARRATOR: Even though
there are around 1, 400

00:02:57.510 --> 00:03:01.200
the thickness of human
hair, stuck end-to-end,

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:03.630
all the viruses on
Earth would stretch

00:03:03.630 --> 00:03:05.370
100 million light years.

00:03:06.760 --> 00:03:10.170
That's 40 times the distance
to our neighboring galaxy,

00:03:10.170 --> 00:03:11.773
Andromeda.

00:03:11.773 --> 00:03:13.140
JANE RADEBAUGH: That
really makes us ask,

00:03:13.140 --> 00:03:15.040
whose planet is this anyway?

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:16.740
If you just added this
up by sheer numbers,

00:03:16.740 --> 00:03:19.650
you would say this
is the virus planet.

00:03:19.650 --> 00:03:23.640
NARRATOR: The microcosmos has
also taken over our bodies.

00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:24.930
We're only partly human.

00:03:24.930 --> 00:03:26.013
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:03:26.013 --> 00:03:27.960
CARLY HOWETT: The fact that
so little of my own body,

00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:30.480
of everyone's body is
as human cells kind

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:33.000
of leaves you wondering, what's
the rest of it made of, right?

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:35.160
 It is astonishing
that we share our bodies

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:38.025
with over 380 trillion viruses.

00:03:38.025 --> 00:03:39.660
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:03:39.660 --> 00:03:42.280
NARRATOR: And over 39
trillion bacterial cells.

00:03:42.280 --> 00:03:43.935
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:03:43.935 --> 00:03:46.320
HAKEEM OLUSEYI: The average
human has more bacterial cells

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:50.950
inside their body than there are
human cells inside their body.

00:03:50.950 --> 00:03:53.190
So what are we really?

00:03:53.190 --> 00:03:54.690
 There are moments in
the history of science

00:03:54.690 --> 00:03:58.090
that I actually remember where I
was when they When for example,

00:03:58.090 --> 00:04:01.050
we detected two colliding black
holes or something like that,

00:04:01.050 --> 00:04:04.410
but another one was when I
realized that almost half

00:04:04.410 --> 00:04:06.930
our body is actually not human.

00:04:06.930 --> 00:04:08.940
 I mean that is crazy.

00:04:08.940 --> 00:04:10.330
 For people that are
scared of bacteria,

00:04:10.330 --> 00:04:12.330
that's a really interesting
thing to know, right?

00:04:12.330 --> 00:04:14.910
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:04:14.910 --> 00:04:18.630
NARRATOR: Bacteria help us
digest food, produce vitamins

00:04:18.630 --> 00:04:20.160
and even influence our mood.

00:04:21.550 --> 00:04:22.350
But what are they?

00:04:24.510 --> 00:04:27.960
Bacteria are single celled
organisms with a cell wall

00:04:27.960 --> 00:04:32.400
and a central nucleus containing
genetic material, RNA or DNA.

00:04:34.988 --> 00:04:36.155
They can move around
and replicate.

00:04:36.155 --> 00:04:39.330
[BLOOD GUSHING]

00:04:39.330 --> 00:04:40.830
Viruses are
completely different.

00:04:40.830 --> 00:04:41.730
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:04:41.730 --> 00:04:42.510
MICHELLE THALLER:
In the universe,

00:04:42.510 --> 00:04:43.643
there are some
mysteries that have

00:04:43.643 --> 00:04:45.840
actually personally
made me kind of pause

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:47.250
and give me goosebumps.

00:04:47.250 --> 00:04:49.110
And strangely enough,
one of them is viruses.

00:04:49.110 --> 00:04:50.860
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:04:50.860 --> 00:04:53.080
The mystery is how
viruses can have

00:04:53.080 --> 00:04:56.920
so much impact on the world
despite their tiny size

00:04:56.920 --> 00:04:57.805
and apparent simplicity.

00:04:57.805 --> 00:05:00.985
[BLOOD GUSHING]

00:05:00.985 --> 00:05:04.030
Most are just a strand of
genetic material sometimes

00:05:04.030 --> 00:05:05.695
encased in a shell of protein.

00:05:05.695 --> 00:05:07.420
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:05:07.420 --> 00:05:09.670
 And compared to
bacteria which behave kind

00:05:09.670 --> 00:05:13.150
of like other normal cells
that we're used to some degree,

00:05:13.150 --> 00:05:16.220
viruses are very strange.

00:05:16.220 --> 00:05:17.980
We don't really
understand viruses.

00:05:17.980 --> 00:05:20.680
We don't even know if
they're alive or not.

00:05:20.680 --> 00:05:23.620
 The question are
viruses alive is

00:05:23.620 --> 00:05:27.040
actually more difficult to
answer than you might think.

00:05:27.040 --> 00:05:29.510
What is the definition of life?

00:05:29.510 --> 00:05:31.342
What does it mean to be alive?

00:05:31.342 --> 00:05:32.142
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:05:32.142 --> 00:05:34.210
NARRATOR: NASA's
definition of life

00:05:34.210 --> 00:05:36.460
is a self-sustained
chemical system

00:05:36.460 --> 00:05:38.440
capable of Darwinian evolution.

00:05:39.760 --> 00:05:44.140
Entities that feed make
energy and reproduce.

00:05:44.140 --> 00:05:46.480
 Viruses can't do
that on their own.

00:05:46.480 --> 00:05:48.970
They have to have a
host cell to do that.

00:05:48.970 --> 00:05:51.310
They have the genetic
material that they

00:05:51.310 --> 00:05:53.420
need but none of the machinery.

00:05:53.420 --> 00:05:58.045
So they really have to infect a
host, if not, they're useless.

00:05:58.045 --> 00:06:00.087
[BLOOD GUSHING]

00:06:00.087 --> 00:06:03.710
MAN: Inside's a host's body,
viruses latch on to the surface

00:06:03.710 --> 00:06:06.915
of cells and then take control.

00:06:08.230 --> 00:06:11.430
 A virus' life
cycle is effectively

00:06:11.430 --> 00:06:13.530
to hack another cell.

00:06:13.530 --> 00:06:16.020
 They have to inject that
genetic material into a living

00:06:16.020 --> 00:06:18.450
cell, and then the cell
is hijacked and starts

00:06:18.450 --> 00:06:20.090
making copies of the virus.

00:06:20.090 --> 00:06:23.460
LEWIS DARTNELL: Which then
have to burst out of this cell

00:06:23.460 --> 00:06:28.350
almost literally like that
gory, grotesque scene in aliens.

00:06:28.350 --> 00:06:30.690
 They burst out
from the inside.

00:06:30.690 --> 00:06:32.850
That's like a cellular
level horrible.

00:06:32.850 --> 00:06:35.760
[BLOOD GUSHING]

00:06:35.760 --> 00:06:37.575
NARRATOR: This method of
replicating is chilling.

00:06:39.480 --> 00:06:42.120
It doesn't answer the question
of whether they're alive.

00:06:45.135 --> 00:06:48.135
That uncertainty may influence
our search for life in space.

00:06:50.283 --> 00:06:51.850
KATHRYN BYWATERS: One of
the interesting things

00:06:51.850 --> 00:06:54.550
to think about is
if we found a virus

00:06:54.550 --> 00:06:58.090
on another planetary surface,
would we classify that as life?

00:06:58.090 --> 00:06:59.770
Would we say that
we have found life?

00:06:59.770 --> 00:07:00.570
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:07:00.570 --> 00:07:02.120
NARRATOR: We don't know.

00:07:02.120 --> 00:07:04.550
But space viruses
could be a sign

00:07:04.550 --> 00:07:06.650
of other extraterrestrial
life forms.

00:07:08.165 --> 00:07:10.770
KATHRYN BYWATERS: Viruses to
have a host cell to replicate.

00:07:10.770 --> 00:07:14.060
So if you find a virus, in
essence you've found life.

00:07:14.060 --> 00:07:16.200
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:07:16.200 --> 00:07:19.510
NARRATOR: Viruses might lead
us to extraterrestrial life.

00:07:19.510 --> 00:07:22.890
Now, new research suggests
that life on Earth

00:07:22.890 --> 00:07:25.660
may have come from
space, brought

00:07:25.660 --> 00:07:31.600
to us in a cosmic bombardment
when our planet was young.

